        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider">

  <!-- Axions -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">The Axion</span>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Why Axions?</h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">The Strong CP Problem</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
            One of the greatest unresolved puzzles in quantum chromodynamics, the theory of the strong force, is why the strong force does not appear to violate charge conjugation and parity symmetry, usually called CP symmetry. Charge conjugation swaps particles' electric charge, while parity reflects a process in a mirror (x,y,z-&gt;-x,-y,-x). CP symmetry means the laws of physics remain unchanged after doing both. QCD contains a theta angle that could allow this symmetry to be violated, but experiments have not found the CP-violating effects that a sizable theta angle would produce, constraining it to be less than about one part in ten billion. Peccei and Quinn proposed a way to make this smallness natural by turning the theta angle from a fixed parameter into a dynamical field. As the universe cools, that field settles toward the value where strong CP violation disappears. The axion is the particle left behind by this mechanism, making it a dark matter candidate that began as a solution to an independent problem in particle physics.
          </p>
          <p>
            In QCD axion models, the axion mass and interaction strength are tied to the Peccei-Quinn symmetry-breaking scale. A higher symmetry-breaking scale produces a lighter, more weakly coupled axion, which is why invisible axion models such as KSVZ and DFSZ can evade ordinary particle searches while remaining theoretically motivated. These models define the QCD axion band, the region of axion parameter space where the particle would solve the strong CP problem. Axion-like particles extend the same experimental idea beyond this band. They can be light, weakly coupled, and serve as the dark matter, but they do not solve strong CP.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Cosmological Production</h3>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
            Axions are compelling as dark matter because their production mechanism gives them a natural way to become cold and abundant. A dark matter candidate must be abundant enough to supply the missing mass inferred from cosmology, and it must be cold enough to clump gravitationally into galaxies and large-scale structure rather than streaming freely through the universe. When Peccei-Quinn symmetry breaks, nothing requires the axion to start at the value that will later cancel strong CP violation. Early cosmic expansion holds it nearly fixed, leaving energy stored in this initial displacement. As the universe cools and QCD effects become important, the axion begins to move toward its preferred value. It rolls, oscillates, and eventually behaves like cold matter on cosmic scales.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
            This origin is different from the production of ordinary particles in a hot thermal bath. The axions that make up the dark matter are not produced through frequent collisions with Standard Model particles. They arise from the collective motion of a field that was already present in the early universe, so even extremely light axions can contribute mass gravitationally without behaving like hot radiation.
          </p>
          <p>
            The details depend on when Peccei-Quinn symmetry breaks relative to inflation. If it breaks after inflation, different regions of the universe begin with different axion values, creating a cosmic patchwork. Boundaries between mismatched regions can form string-like and wall-like defects, whose later decay adds axions to the population produced by misalignment. If the symmetry breaks before or during inflation and is not restored afterward, inflation stretches one initial axion value across the observable universe, removing this visible patchwork but making the abundance depend on that single starting value. This pre-inflationary picture is especially important for very light, high-scale axions. In that regime, the axion wavelength is larger than the detector itself, which is precisely the condition that allows CAL and DMRadio to search for the coherent field with a lumped-element circuit instead of a wavelength-sized microwave cavity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider">

  <!-- Axion-photon coupling -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">Detection Principle</span>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Axion-Photon Coupling</h2>
      <div class="two-col-60">
        <div>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
            The axion-photon coupling gives experiments a way to search for a particle that otherwise barely interacts with ordinary matter. An axion will not leave a charged track in a detector, but a strong magnetic field can convert part of the axion field into an electromagnetic response. This conversion is known as the inverse Primakoff effect, and it is the reason magnets are central to many axion searches.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
            The coupling is often summarized by a term proportional to a&nbsp;&mathbf{E}&middot;&mathbf{B}, linking the axion field to electric and magnetic fields. For dark matter axions, the signal is better understood as a wave passing through the laboratory than as a sequence of individual collisions. Because the axions are extremely light and numerous, the local dark matter field oscillates coherently. When that field overlaps with a static magnetic field, the axion-photon interaction makes the detector behave as though a tiny alternating current is flowing along the magnetic field lines. That effective current produces a real electromagnetic signal whose frequency is set by the axion mass.
          </p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
            The appropriate detector design changes with the axion mass. At higher masses, microwave cavity haloscopes can enhance the signal when a cavity mode matches the axion frequency. At lower masses, the axion wavelength becomes too large for a practical cavity. Lumped-element detectors avoid this scaling problem by treating the experiment as an electrical circuit. The axion-induced response couples into a pickup structure, a resonant LC circuit enhances the chosen frequency, and a SQUID or other quantum sensor reads out the tiny current or magnetic flux.
          </p>
          <p>
            CAL and DMRadio apply this low-mass strategy in a solenoidal geometry, where the axion-induced response is coupled into a pickup and measured with sensitive quantum electronics. These experiments target the low-mass region where the axion can behave as a coherent wave and where larger instruments can move toward the QCD axion band.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="diagram-placeholder">
            <p><strong>Exclusion plot</strong></p>
            <p>g<sub>a&gamma;&gamma;</sub> vs m<sub>a</sub></p>
            <p style="font-size:0.8rem;margin-top:0.5rem;">Replace with sensitivity projection figure</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider">

  <!-- Landscape -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="section-label">Experimental Landscape</span>
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">DMRadio in Context</h2>
      <p style="margin-bottom:2.5rem;max-width:680px;">
        Multiple experiments are pursuing axion dark matter across complementary mass ranges.
        DMRadio targets the sub-&mu;eV window not accessible to cavity-based haloscopes like ADMX.
      </p>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h4 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;color:var(--blue);">DMRadio (this experiment)</h4>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;">Lumped-element solenoidal resonator. Target: [X&ndash;Y] &mu;eV. Quantum-limited SQUID readout.</p>
          <div style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><span class="tag tag-blue">Sub-&mu;eV</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;color:var(--blue);">ADMX</h4>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;">Microwave cavity haloscope at University of Washington. Currently covering 2&ndash;40 &mu;eV.</p>
          <div style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><span class="tag">&mu;eV range</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;color:var(--blue);">HAYSTAC</h4>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;">Yale/Berkeley/Colorado experiment. Higher-frequency cavity search with squeezed-state receiver.</p>
          <div style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><span class="tag">10&ndash;100 &mu;eV</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;color:var(--blue);">ABRACADABRA</h4>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;">MIT solenoidal broadband search; demonstrated detection principle used by DMRadio.</p>
          <div style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><span class="tag">Sub-&mu;eV</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;color:var(--blue);">CASPEr</h4>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;">NMR-based axion search via axion-nucleon coupling. Complementary to photon-coupling searches.</p>
          <div style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><span class="tag">Ultra-low mass</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4 style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;color:var(--blue);">SHAFT / AION</h4>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;">Atom interferometry-based searches for ultralight dark matter and gravitational waves.</p>
          <div style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><span class="tag">Ultra-light</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="divider">

